course: world
lesson: 51
title: Forces — pushes, pulls, friction and magnets
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A force is a push or a pull. Forces can start something moving, stop it, speed it up, slow it down, turn it or change its shape.
explain: When you kick a ball you push it, and when you open a drawer you pull it. A bigger force makes a bigger change.
explain: Friction is a force that appears when two surfaces rub against each other. It always works against the movement, so it slows things down and turns some of the motion into heat.
explain: Rough surfaces make more friction than smooth ones: a sledge slides far on smooth ice but stops quickly on grass or gravel.
explain: Gravity is the pull of the Earth that makes things fall and gives them weight. Magnets pull on iron and steel, and they can pull or push other magnets without touching them.
fact: A force is a push or a pull.
fact: A force can change how fast something moves, the direction it moves or its shape.
fact: Friction acts between surfaces that rub and works against the movement.
fact: Rough surfaces make more friction than smooth surfaces.
fact: Rubbing your hands together makes them warm because friction turns motion into heat.
fact: Oil and water on a surface reduce friction, which is why wet floors are slippery.
fact: Gravity is the pull of the Earth that makes things fall downwards.
fact: Air resistance is a kind of friction from the air; a parachute uses it to fall slowly.
fact: Every magnet has a north pole and a south pole.
fact: Opposite poles of two magnets attract and like poles repel.
fact: Magnets attract iron, steel, nickel and cobalt, but not wood, plastic, glass, gold, copper or aluminium, which drinks cans are made of.
fact: A magnetic force can act through air, paper and water without touching.
exercise: What is a force? | A push or a pull | A kind of energy stored in food | Something only living things can make | The speed at which an object moves
exercise: Which force slows down a ball rolling across the grass? | Friction | Magnetism | Gravity pulling it forwards | Electricity
exercise: Why does a sledge slide further on ice than on grass? | Ice is smooth, so there is less friction | Ice pulls the sledge with a magnetic force | Grass is lighter than ice | Ice makes the sledge heavier
exercise: Why do your hands get warm when you rub them together? | Friction turns the motion into heat | Your blood flows out into your fingers | The air around them becomes hotter | Your skin makes a little electricity
exercise: Which force makes an apple fall from a tree? | Gravity | Friction | Air resistance | Magnetism
exercise: What happens when you bring the north pole of one magnet near the south pole of another? | They attract each other | They push each other away | Nothing happens at all | They both lose their magnetism
exercise: What happens when two north poles are brought close together? | They repel each other | They attract each other | They stick firmly together | They turn into south poles
exercise: Which of these will a magnet pick up? | A steel paper clip | A copper coin | An aluminium drinks can | A wooden pencil
exercise: How does a parachute help a person land safely? | It catches a lot of air, so air resistance slows the fall | It switches off gravity for a while | It makes the person lighter | It pushes the ground away
exercise: Why are wet floors slippery? | Water between the shoe and the floor reduces friction | Water makes the floor pull your feet | Water makes your shoes heavier | Water makes gravity stronger
exercise: Which of these is a pull? | Opening a drawer towards you | Kicking a football | Pressing a doorbell | Shoving a heavy box away from you
exercise: Which surface gives the most friction for a rolling toy car? | A thick rough carpet | A polished wooden floor | A sheet of smooth ice | A glossy tiled floor
exercise: Can a magnet attract a paper clip through a sheet of paper? | Yes, magnetic force acts through paper | No, magnets work only on touch | Only if the paper is wet | Only if the paper is made of iron
exercise: What does friction always do to a moving object? | It works against the movement | It speeds the object up | It pulls the object downwards | It makes the object magnetic
test: A cyclist stops pedalling on a flat road. Why does the bicycle slowly stop? | Friction and air resistance slow it down | Gravity pulls it backwards along the road | The bicycle runs out of energy inside it | The road attracts it like a magnet
test: Why do football boots have studs on the sole? | To grip the ground and increase friction | To make the boots lighter to run in | To reduce friction so the players can slide | To stop gravity pulling the player down
test: Two magnets lie on a table and slide away from each other when brought close. What must be facing each other? | Two like poles, such as south and south | A north pole and a south pole | A pole and the middle of the other magnet | Nothing, magnets always move apart
test: You have a mixture of iron nails and sand. What is the easiest way to separate them? | Pull the nails out with a magnet | Pour water over the mixture | Shake the mixture in a jar | Blow on it with a fan
test: Which is true about friction? | It works against movement and makes heat | It only exists between surfaces made of metal | It always helps things move faster | It is the same thing as gravity
test: Why do people put oil on a squeaky bicycle chain? | Oil reduces the friction between the moving parts | Oil makes the metal magnetic | Oil makes the chain heavier and stronger | Oil increases the grip of the chain
test: You squeeze a lump of clay and it becomes flat. What did the force do? | It changed the shape of the clay | It changed the weight of the clay | It made the clay magnetic | It made the clay lighter than air
test: If there were no friction at all, what would happen when you tried to walk? | Your feet would slip and you could not move forward | You would walk much faster than before, without effort | You would float up into the air | Nothing would change at all
test: Which of these does a magnet NOT attract? | A gold ring | A steel spoon | An iron nail | A nickel washer
test: why's the floor so slippy after mopping | Water reduces friction between your shoes and the floor | The water pushes your feet away | The mop makes the floor magnetic | Wet floors pull you down harder
test: tried to stick a magnet on a coke can, didnt work, why | Aluminium is not magnetic | The can was too cold | The magnet was upside down | Magnets only stick to wood
test: my brother says heavy things fall cos the air pushes them down. true? | No, gravity, the pull of the Earth, makes them fall | Yes, air pushes down on everything | Yes, without air nothing would fall | No, things fall because they are heavy inside
test: Mia reckons two north poles of magnets stick together. right? | No, like poles repel; opposite poles attract | Yes, the same poles always attract each other | Yes, but only big magnets | No, magnets never push apart
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the phenomenon designated as friction? | A force arising between surfaces in contact which invariably opposes their relative motion | A force arising between surfaces in contact which invariably assists their relative motion | A force exerted solely by magnetised bodies upon ferrous materials | A force by which the Earth draws all bodies towards its centre
exam: It is given that a sledge is propelled with equal force across ice and across grass. Identify the principal reason for its greater displacement upon the ice. | The smoothness of the ice occasions a lesser frictional resistance | The ice exerts an attractive force upon the runners of the sledge | The gravitational pull of the Earth is diminished above ice | The mass of the sledge is reduced whilst upon a frozen surface
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the interaction of two magnets whose like poles are placed in proximity. | The magnets shall repel one another | The magnets shall attract one another | The magnets shall be wholly unaffected by one another | The magnets shall forfeit their magnetic properties
exam: Whereas a magnet is applied in turn to the objects hereinafter enumerated, determine which object shall be attracted thereto. | A fastening of steel | A coin of copper | A receptacle of aluminium | A ring of gold
